  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Attention specializes on query length, not only T",
      "items": [
        "Decode 1 is B=1, L=1, T=1. Verify 1 is B=1, L=8, T=8. A different workload B=8, L=1 also has T=8 but uses the L1 attention arithmetic.",
        "The short-token native layer uses 16 query heads, two KV heads and head dimension 64. L8 queries pass through F16 staging before E4M3 packing because QDIRECT=0."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "One fused attention launch, several internal phases",
      "items": [
        "Preparation, cache writes, split-KV attention, reduction and final norm/gate belong to the native attention launch. These boxes are internal operations, not separate Python launches.",
        "L1 uses F16 mma.sync after unpacking FP8 K/V. The selected L8 path uses E4M3 WGMMA for both QK and PV, with FP32 accumulators. Older F16-PV comments describe a different branch."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Planning and retained boundaries",
      "items": [
        "The launch plan depends on B, L, window, page count and KV pairs. Full decode1 has a pinned 252-split, eight-copy cooperative plan; sliding and verify use their applicable planner rows.",
        "The attention vector rounds to BF16 before its output RMS and sigmoid gate. Paged-cache index limits and non-unit scaled L8 fallback can select QK prep plus FA3. That fallback is outside the native close-up."
      ]
    }
  ]
